===Etymology===
From {{compound|en|snake|fly}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Any insect of the order {{taxfmt|Raphidioptera|order}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1997|author=Charles S. Henry|chapter=11: Modern mating systems in archaic Holometabola: sexuality in neuropterid insects|editors=Jae C. Choe; Bernard J. Crespi|title=The Evolution of Mating Systems in Insects and Arachnids|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=X3Iz-zXolVcC&pg=PA196&dq=%22snakefly%22%7C%22snakeflies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=KdaIUuXiKYH_iAfHw4DQDg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22snakefly%22%7C%22snakeflies%22&f=false|page=196
|passage=Although sexual behavior is not well studied in the order, observations exist for members of both '''snakefly''' families.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=David Hugh Kattes|title=Insects of Texas: a Practical Guide|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=BRCBQwjhC8kC&pg=PA109&dq=%22snakefly%22%7C%22snakeflies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=KdaIUuXiKYH_iAfHw4DQDg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22snakefly%22%7C%22snakeflies%22&f=false|page=109
|passage=Adult '''snakeflies''' stalk their prey in a manner similar to snakes. When an insect comes within reach, the '''snakefly''' will strike, catching the victim with its jaws.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Jogeir N. Stokland; Juha Siitonen; Bengt Gunnar Jonsson|title=Biodiversity in Dead Wood|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=3R4i5HKMfxEC&pg=PA239&dq=%22snakefly%22%7C%22snakeflies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=KdaIUuXiKYH_iAfHw4DQDg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22snakefly%22%7C%22snakeflies%22&f=false|page=239
|passage=The order '''snakeflies''' (Raphidioptera) consists predominantly of saproxylic predators (Aspöck, 2002). '''Snakeflies''' have an almost worldwide distribution, mostly occurring in temperate regions and in mountainous areas closer to the equator.}}

